1. In the code below, identify the object and the method.
             df.head()

The object is the dataframe (df) and the method is head().

2. Is `df.shape` an attribute or a method?

df.shape is an attribute (no parenthesis means not a method call).

3. If `df.shape` returns `(75, 8)`, explain what both numbers mean.

(75, 8) means 75 columns and 8 rows; shape will report (rows, columns) (INVERSE!)

4. What type of pandas object is usually returned by `df["Data_Value"]`?

A series is normally returned, which looks like a single column with an index.

5. What type of pandas object is usually returned by `df[["County", "Data_Value"]]`?

A data frame's list of column names (double brackets) will return a 2-D dataframe, even if the list has a singluar name.

parks = pd.DataFrame({"Park":["Oak","Pine","Maple","Cedar"], "Area":[12,25,18,40], "Visitors":[120,250,180,310]})

6. Write the expression that selects only the `Visitors` column from the `parks` data frame.

parks["Visitors"]

7. Write the expression that selects only `Park` and `Visitors` from the `parks` data frame.

parks[["Park", "Visitors"]]

8. What does `parks.iloc[0]` return?

This indicates the first row (as position 0) and returns as a series where the index is the column names.

9. What rows are selected by `parks.iloc[1:4]`? 

Positions 1,2,3 and not 4 or 0. This is because the first number in the brackets indicates position 1 as the starting point and position 4 as the end point, and does not include the end point to be indexed.

10. Write one expression that selects rows 2 through 4 by position and then keeps only `Park` and `Area`.

parks.iloc[2:5][["Park", "Area"]]

11. Write a Boolean filter that keeps only parks with `Area > 20`.

parks[parks["Area"] > 20]

12. What rows would remain after that filter?

Pine (25) and Cedar (40). Oak (12) and Maple(18) will return False when met with the condition of >20.

13. Create a new DataFrame named `large_parks` containing those rows and make it an independent copy.

large_parks = parks[parks["Area"] > 20].copy() 

14. Why might `.copy()` be useful after filtering?

Filtering can return a view which is tied to the original data frame, so copy() provides an independent object able to be modified.

15. Write one line that changes all three column names to `Name`, `Area_ha`, and `Annual_Visitors` using `df.columns = [...]`.

parks.column = ["Name", "Area_ha", "Annual_Visitors"]

16. What problem occurs if the list assigned to `.columns` has only two names while the DataFrame has three columns?

A ValueError: Length mismatch occurs. The numbers of name need to exactly equal the number of columns. 

values = pd.Series(["12.5", "18.0", "missing", "22.5"])
17. Why might pandas store these values as strings rather than numbers?

An entry, "missing", is not a numeric text. So if any value in a column can't be a number, Pandas stores the entire column as an object.

18. Write code using `pd.to_numeric(..., errors="coerce")` to convert the Series.

values = pd.to_numeric(values, errors = "coerce")

19. What happens to the value `"missing"` when `errors="coerce"` is used?

Since "missing" is unable to parsed as a number, errors = "coerce" will turn it into "NaN" which means missing. It will not raise an error.

20. What does `.isna().sum()` tell you?

The number of NaN values is counted per column for a dataframe, or the total for a series.

21. Explain the difference between `dropna()` and `fillna()`.

dropna() will remove rows (or columns) which contain missing values. 
fillna(value) keeps them but replaces the missing values with something that can be specified.

22. Write code that sorts `parks` from the highest to lowest number of visitors.

parks.sort_values("Visitors", ascending=False)

23. Write code to calculate the mean and median of `Visitors`.

parks["Visitors"].mean()
parks["Visitors"].median()

24. In a strongly right-skewed distribution, which is usually larger: the mean or the median? Why?

The mean is usually larger. A long right tail (which means few very large values) will pull the mean up. 

25. In a histogram, what does `bins=10` control?

The number of bins says how many intervals the data range is split into (like how many bars does a historgram have?) More bins means finer details typically.

26. What is `ax` in `ax = df["Value"].plot(kind="hist")`?

A Matplotlib Axes object is a plotting surface that will be truend by .plot(). Ax will capture this so titles, labels, and other customizations can be added to a specific plot.

27. Write the Matplotlib commands that set a title, x-axis label, and y-axis label on `ax`.

ax.set_title("Distribution of Value")
ax.set_xlabel("Value")
ax.set_ylabel("Frequency")

28. Explain the difference between equal interval and quantile classification.

Equal interval will split data ranges into classes of equal width, regardless of how many observations will appear in each.
Quantile will aggregate the data into similar numbers of observations for each class, so class counts will be balanced but the width of each class will vary.

29. Suppose `values = [5, 12, 3, 20]`. Write a short Python loop that prints only values greater than 10. Then write the pandas-style Boolean condition you would use if the same values were in a Series called `s`.

values = [5,12,3,20]
for v in values:
    if v > 10:
        print(v)

s[s > 10]